// src/audit_wire.pdx — mount.pdxfs.M3-002 (issue #8) — libpdx-audit
// INTENT/RESULT record pair, sharing one audit_id, around every
// sys_mount attempt.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// libpdx-audit @0.2 API this file wires against
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `libpdx-audit` (paideia-satellites/libpdx-audit, @0.2, commit
// bfc63a5) exposes a `module AuditClient` with, among others:
//
//   audit_begin(op_name_ptr, op_args_ptr) -> u64
//     rdi = op_name_ptr  (NUL-terminated, copied into a 32-byte wire
//                         field -- longer strings truncated by the
//                         library's own marshalling helper)
//     rsi = op_args_ptr  (NUL-terminated, copied into a 128-byte wire
//                         field, same truncation contract)
//     returns a wire audit_id ((pid<<32)|local_id) on success, or 0 on
//     failure (a state gate or a send failure -- see "forgiving
//     posture" below)
//
//   audit_commit(audit_id, exit_code) -> u64
//     rdi = audit_id     (the value audit_begin returned)
//     rsi = exit_code
//     returns AUDIT_OK (0) or an AUDIT_ERR_* code -- this file's own
//     `mount_audit_commit` ignores the return value (see below)
//
// Both are cross-repo bare-label calls (`call audit_begin;`,
// `call audit_commit;`), the identical mechanism `src/volume_cap.pdx`
// already uses for `vol_kind_narrow` -- see that file's own header
// §"libpdx-volume link status" for why no `extern`/import syntax is
// needed.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// "Shared audit_id" across INTENT and RESULT — how this issue's own
// wording maps onto the real API
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This issue's task text describes "emit INTENT via audit_begin(...)
// returning audit_id" before the sys_mount call, then "emit RESULT via
// audit_commit(..., parent_audit_id=<INTENT id>)" after. A full read of
// `libpdx-audit`'s real `audit_client.pdx` finds no `parent_audit_id`
// parameter on `audit_commit` at all -- that name belongs to a
// DIFFERENT entry point, `AuditClient::audit_set_parent(parent_audit_
// id)`, which links a CHILD process's own fresh audit to some OTHER
// process's audit tree (e.g. a shell's `ShellCommandRecord` propagated
// to a spawned child) -- not the INTENT/RESULT pairing this issue
// actually wants, which is two lifecycle events on the SAME audit.
// `audit_commit`'s real second argument is `exit_code`, not an id.
//
// The real API already gives this issue exactly what it is asking for,
// via a DIFFERENT mechanism than the one its own text assumed:
// `audit_begin`'s return value IS the shared correlation key --
// `audit_commit(audit_id, exit_code)` takes that SAME id back and the
// library's own state machine (`record_audit_id`, checked by both
// `audit_record_output` and `audit_commit` via an explicit `cmp rax,
// rdi; jne AUDIT_ERR_ID_MISMATCH` gate) is what proves the RESULT event
// is the same audit as the INTENT event. This file therefore calls
// `audit_begin` once (mirroring `mkfs.pdxfs.M3-004`'s own `audit_wire.
// pdx`, unchanged in shape) and threads its return value, unmodified,
// into `audit_commit` once the mount pipeline's real outcome is known --
// there is no second, separate "parent" id to plumb, and no call to
// `audit_set_parent` (this tool has no parent audit context of its own
// to accept either; a future shell-spawn integration is out of this
// issue's scope).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// op_name literals — a genuine per-invocation signal, not a placeholder
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `mount_audit_begin` selects one of two op_name literals by the
// caller's own `--dry-run` flag, mirroring `mkfs.pdxfs.M3-004`'s own
// force-flag split exactly: `"mount.pdxfs.mount"` for a real (non-dry-
// run) invocation, `"mount.pdxfs.mount.dry_run"` when `--dry-run` was
// given -- so the audit trail distinguishes a real mount attempt from a
// preview with no kernel-side effect, without needing a dedicated
// UEJ_KIND (none exists for this purpose; see the "no retain-forever
// UEJ_KIND" note mkfs.pdxfs's own audit_wire.pdx carries, unchanged
// finding, re-confirmed for this landing). `op_args` is the caller's
// actual `<mount-point>` string -- the real argv value, truncated to
// 128 bytes by libpdx-audit's own marshalling helper if longer, not
// this file's concern.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Forgiving posture: every outcome is non-fatal
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Per this issue's own instruction ("audit_client call may return
// quickly with a documented no-op -- that's fine, just log it and
// continue"), matching mkfs.pdxfs's own audit_wire.pdx precisely:
// `mount_audit_begin` returns whatever `audit_begin` returns, including
// 0 on failure, with NO error branch of its own -- a 0 audit_id is a
// valid "auditing did not happen" sentinel `main.pdx` threads through
// unexamined. `mount_audit_commit` treats `audit_id == 0` as "nothing
// was opened, nothing to commit" and skips the call entirely (calling
// `audit_commit(0, ...)` would hit `AUDIT_ERR_ID_MISMATCH` inside
// libpdx-audit for no benefit); for a non-zero audit_id, `audit_
// commit`'s return value is discarded. This matches libpdx-audit's own
// STATUS.md note that the kernel-side audit-journal broker dispatch is
// still stubbed -- calling into a stubbed daemon and getting back a
// quick no-op is an EXPECTED outcome at this landing, not an error
// condition to surface.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder-gap discipline
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic. Reserved-label
// discipline: every branch label in this file is prefixed `mount_audit_`.
//
// Upstream: paideia-satellites/libpdx-audit @0.2 (commit bfc63a5),
// src/audit_client.pdx (AuditClient::audit_begin/audit_commit/
// audit_set_parent); mkfs.pdxfs's own src/audit_wire.pdx (M3-004, #11 --
// the sibling-repo precedent this file's shape, forgiving posture, and
// write-up structure mirror).

module AuditWire = structure {

  // --- op_name literals (see module header for why two, and why they
  //     are the real per-invocation signal rather than a placeholder).
  //     Lengths verified via `python3 -c "print(len(...))"`. -------------
  pub let mount_audit_lit_op_name          : [u8; 18] = "mount.pdxfs.mount\0"
  pub let mount_audit_lit_op_name_dry_run  : [u8; 26] = "mount.pdxfs.mount.dry_run\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // mount_audit_begin(mount_point_ptr, dry_run_flag) -> u64
  //   (the wire audit_id AuditClient::audit_begin returned, or 0 -- see
  //   module header's "forgiving posture" note; never examined by any
  //   branch in this function)
  //
  //   rdi = mount_point_ptr  (NUL-terminated <mount-point> string,
  //                           passed through verbatim as audit_begin's
  //                           op_args)
  //   rsi = dry_run_flag     (0, or any nonzero value -- main.pdx passes
  //                           the raw `flags & Argv::PA_FLAG_DRY_RUN`
  //                           mask, either 0 or 8, matching this repo's
  //                           own cross-file "pass the masked bits
  //                           unnormalised, gate on != 0" convention)
  //
  // No push/pop needed: the only value that must survive past a
  // register-clobbering event is mount_point_ptr, and the one `call
  // audit_begin` below is a plain SysV `call` (not a `syscall` trap --
  // the kernel's #743 info-leak hardening zeroing caller-save registers
  // applies only to the `syscall` instruction itself, not to an
  // ordinary cross-repo `call`), so staging mount_point_ptr in the
  // caller-save `rax` register across the branch below (before the
  // call, not across it) is safe.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let mount_audit_begin : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched} =
    fn (mount_point_ptr: u64) (dry_run_flag: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap, sched},
      justification: "mount.pdxfs.M3-002 (#8): mount_audit_begin entry point. rax = mount_point_ptr (staged from rdi before rdi is repurposed for the op_name literal pointer below -- safe, no syscall trap intervenes). `cmp rsi, 0; je mount_audit_begin_no_dry_run` selects mount_audit_lit_op_name (real mount) when the incoming dry_run_flag is zero, else mount_audit_lit_op_name_dry_run; each arm does `lea rdi, [rip + <literal>]` then falls into mount_audit_begin_have_name. `mov rsi, rax` (op_args = mount_point_ptr, restored from staging). `call audit_begin` (libpdx-audit, cross-repo bare-label call -- same mechanism as src/volume_cap.pdx's `call vol_kind_narrow`). Its return value (rax) IS this function's return value verbatim -- no examination, per module header's forgiving posture. No `add reg,[mem]`. No `test`. Labels prefixed mount_audit_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;                       // stage mount_point_ptr

        cmp rsi, 0;
        je mount_audit_begin_no_dry_run;

        lea rdi, [rip + mount_audit_lit_op_name_dry_run];
        jmp mount_audit_begin_have_name;

      mount_audit_begin_no_dry_run:
        lea rdi, [rip + mount_audit_lit_op_name];

      mount_audit_begin_have_name:
        mov rsi, rax;                       // op_args = mount_point_ptr
        call audit_begin;
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // mount_audit_commit(audit_id, exit_code) -> ()
  //
  //   rdi = audit_id    (mount_audit_begin's return value -- 0 means "no
  //                       audit record was opened", see below)
  //   rsi = exit_code   (the real process exit status this invocation is
  //                       about to return)
  //
  // audit_id == 0 short-circuits to a no-op (nothing to commit); a
  // non-zero audit_id calls AuditClient::audit_commit and discards its
  // return value entirely, per module header's forgiving posture. This
  // IS the "RESULT record, correlated to the INTENT record by the
  // shared audit_id" this issue's own text asks for -- see module
  // header's "shared audit_id" note for why no separate parent_audit_id
  // parameter exists or is needed.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let mount_audit_commit : (u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched} =
    fn (audit_id: u64) (exit_code: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap, sched},
      justification: "mount.pdxfs.M3-002 (#8): mount_audit_commit entry point. `cmp rdi, 0; je mount_audit_commit_skip` -- a zero audit_id (mount_audit_begin's own failure/no-op sentinel) means nothing was opened, so there is nothing to commit; calling AuditClient::audit_commit(0, ...) would only produce a meaningless AUDIT_ERR_ID_MISMATCH inside libpdx-audit. Otherwise `call audit_commit` with rdi/rsi already in the correct (audit_id, exit_code) position from this function's own incoming arguments -- no register shuffling needed. Return value discarded (no `mov`, the call's rax is simply never read) per module header's forgiving posture. No `add reg,[mem]`. No `test`. Labels prefixed mount_audit_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je mount_audit_commit_skip;

        call audit_commit;

      mount_audit_commit_skip:
        ret;
      }
    }
}
